WEBVTT

00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:35.387
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Saturday, November 21st, 2026. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:43.891
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking at how small councils pay for what they already own, and who gets a say when the money comes from somewhere else.

00:00:44.091 --> 00:00:45.219
<v Riley>Starting with the news.

00:00:46.619 --> 00:00:56.955
<v Riley>Most of today's stories are close to home, and most are about people doing ordinary civic and community work. A new provincial government is taking shape over the top of all of it.

00:00:58.517 --> 00:01:30.195
<v Casey>Williams Lake wants residents' input on its parks and trails. The city is updating its Parks and Trails Plan, which dates to 2011, and council approved $150,000 for the work in the 2025 Capital Budget. City planner Jessica Ball's council report says the new plan will set a long-term vision and a strategic framework for capital investment, operations, maintenance, programming and stewardship. At the Tuesday, November 17th council meeting, Coun. Scott Nelson said he hopes the waterfront redevelopment is included.

00:01:30.645 --> 00:01:34.397
<v Riley>The current plan is fifteen years old, so this update is overdue.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:02:03.594
<v Riley>The Made in the Cariboo Christmas Market ran Saturday, November 14th, at the Williams Lake Tourism Discovery Centre. More than 700 people came through, looking at 28 vendors spread across all three floors. The new face this year was Chad Marchand, a professional forester with BC Timber Sales. He makes end-grain hardwood cutting boards in his family's backyard shop, seals them with beeswax, and started after work about five or six months ago.

00:02:04.044 --> 00:02:10.996
<v Casey>Seven hundred people in one building, in a city of about 10,700. That's a real crowd for a November Saturday.

00:02:12.508 --> 00:02:49.890
<v Riley>Ever After Attire's Borrowed Magic pop-up runs today, Saturday, November 21st, from noon to 3 p.m. in the Graham Kelsey Room at the Central Cariboo Arts and Culture Centre. Graduates can try on dresses and suits and take one home for free. No registration is needed, and a friend or parent can come along for fittings. The lending program is open to graduates in the Williams Lake area regardless of financial circumstances. Program coordinator Nikki Blodgett took over from photographer Rick Magnell about a year ago. The collection holds about 125 dresses, plus suits, shirts, ties, shoes and heels.

00:02:51.368 --> 00:03:20.016
<v Casey>The 100 Mile House Santa Claus Parade has 53 floats entered. The South Cariboo Chamber of Commerce is running it on Friday, November 27th, starting at 5:30 p.m. on Birch Avenue. This year's theme is a Griswold Family Christmas. Chamber president Jamie Gauvin said the group had over 40 floats last year. Before the parade, Santa and Mrs. Claus fly into the 100 Mile House Airport between 3:30 and 4 p.m. and hand out goodie bags to anyone who comes to meet them.

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:26.450
<v Riley>Fifty-three floats means fifty-three households building something in a garage over the next week.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:51.046
<v Riley>Williams Lake's Tyson Delay placed second in the 105 kilogram class at the Canadian Strongman Championships in Quebec in early October. About 17 men were in his class, which he described as the most competitive in the country. Over three days he competed in six events. In the one-arm dumbbell event, he got four reps on the heavy weight, and that earned him second place.

00:03:52.642 --> 00:04:42.480
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Tribune ran three stories on the new B.C. government, and together they describe one event. Lt.-Gov. Wendy Cocchia swore in Lorne Doerkson as the province's 38th premier on Friday morning, November 20th. The B.C. Conservatives won 64 of 93 seats in the October 24th election. On Monday, November 16th, Doerkson said in a written statement that there would be no fall legislative session. He says a tax cut will take effect as soon as January 1st. SOGI 123, the anti-bullying program in schools, will be ended immediately, though he hasn't yet explained how that works in practice. A review of the province's finances starts in the coming weeks under Finance Minister Peter Milobar. Repealing the Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples Act waits until the spring session, and Doerkson says that repeal is still the plan.

00:04:42.929 --> 00:04:48.337
<v Riley>The spring session is where the hard ones land. Everything else in that list is a timetable.

00:04:49.958 --> 00:05:16.110
<v Riley>Interim B.C. NDP Leader Jennifer Whiteside has written to Doerkson asking the incoming government to oppose Quebecor's $31-billion bid to take over Telus. Her argument is that combining two national players would mean fewer choices, weaker service, and higher monthly bills for families and small businesses. Telus owns Public Mobile and Quebecor owns Freedom Mobile, so both companies sell budget cell service in B.C.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.960
<v Casey>The phone bill is what families will notice first.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:46.352
<v Casey>On November 13th, the U.S. Federal Aviation Administration called a full ground stop for all planes after telecommunications outages hit its systems. About 3,600 flights were cancelled. On November 14th, Delta cancelled 20 per cent of its flights, United cancelled 17 per cent, and American cancelled 15 per cent. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy said the problem was an incredibly old air traffic control system.

00:05:46.802 --> 00:05:50.514
<v Riley>One outage, one country's airspace, and an old system blamed for it.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:06:30.666
<v Riley>Nazko First Nation members voted unanimously to add the Three Nations Store and Lodge property to the Nation's reserve land base. In the November referendum, every vote cast supported the proposal. The property at 9480 Nazko Road has a general store, a gas station, cabins, and campsites. Chief Leah Stump said the businesses serve the people who live, work, and travel in the Nazko Valley, and that reserve status will help the Nation plan for their future. Nazko has pursued the addition since 2021. The proposal still needs federal review before the land can receive reserve status.

00:06:31.116 --> 00:06:35.460
<v Casey>A community votes, the vote is unanimous, and the final word still sits in Ottawa.

00:06:37.291 --> 00:06:59.491
<v Casey>Bob Zimmerman, former longtime president of Quesnel Search and Rescue, has received the national Emergency Management Exemplary Service Award. Bob Hollowenko, the group's current vice president, says the award required proof of long service and exemplary work. Zimmerman has been with the team for 37 years and led in nearly every search discipline. Many current members trained under him.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.316
<v Riley>Hollowenko says everyone who goes through the team leaves with a skill they learned from Zimmerman.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:42.280
<v Riley>Operation Christmas Child shoeboxes are due at Evangelical Free Church in Williams Lake by Sunday, November 22nd. Kathy Shetler, who has been involved since the mid-1990s, says the city shipped 451 boxes in 2025, with about 50 more donated online. She hopes to match or beat that. Samaritan's Purse runs the project through local churches and ministries overseas. Donors can pack for children aged two to four, five to nine, or 10 to 14. Shetler asks people to leave out candy, liquids, toothpaste, soap, and glass.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.570
<v Casey>The deadline is tomorrow, so it's a last-minute drop-off.

00:07:47.197 --> 00:08:18.195
<v Casey>Canadian Armed Forces search and rescue crews from 442 Transport and Rescue Squadron, based at 19 Wing Comox, trained with flares over the Chilcotin Military Training Area, about 50 kilometres southwest of Williams Lake. The exercises ran from November 16th to 20th, each night from 6 p.m. to 1 a.m. CC-295 Kingfisher aircraft dropped smoke and illumination flares that drifted down to the water and burned for four to five minutes at a time. The training keeps crews ready for safe nighttime rescues.

00:08:18.645 --> 00:08:23.149
<v Riley>If you saw white lights over the Chilcotin this week, that was the training.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:52.690
<v Riley>Cranbrook's council has received an update on the city's facilities. The estimated cost of projects at city facilities over the next ten years is over $40 million. Western Financial Place has the largest share, at $8.3 million, for renovations, upgrades to the ammonia exhaust systems, and arena dehumidification. The public works building needs almost $5 million in projects, including operations and workforce upgrades and roofing renewal.

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:57.827
<v Casey>Cranbrook's list is long. It's the same kind of list the Williams Lake planners are working from.

00:08:58.277 --> 00:09:06.277
<v Riley>Parks plans, arena repairs, a ten-year list. Those bills arrive whether or not anyone budgeted for them. First, our community partner.

00:09:07.677 --> 00:09:29.957
<v Casey>It's National Addictions Awareness Week, and CMHA Cariboo Chilcotin is one of the places to start in Williams Lake and 100 Mile House. They offer mental health counselling, housing support, victim services, and crisis intervention. Their Jubilee House helps residents connect with housing, benefits, and treatment. If you're not sure where to begin, call them or check cariboo.cmha.bc.ca.

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:36.872
<v Riley>Jubilee House matters for people who need a bed before they need a plan. Good to see it named this week.

00:09:36.972 --> 00:09:40.500
<v Riley>Okay, the deep dive. Barriere's money problem, from the top.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:10:02.484
<v Riley>Barriere's mayor says his town has about 1,000 ratepayers and multi-million dollar projects to fund, and he calls that near impossible. So when a town that size can't cover its own infrastructure, does taking the problem to a regional association give its council real control? Or do the decisions stay with whoever holds the grants?

00:10:02.766 --> 00:10:08.542
<v Casey>Start with the asset work. Most small councils don't look at their pipes and roads until something breaks.

00:10:09.058 --> 00:10:26.194
<v Riley>Mayor Rob Kerslake says that as Barriere digs into asset management, the picture gets worse. The council now has the number before it votes. That's the honest version of the process. The research puts it at 11 kilometres of aging water lines, 27 kilometres of roads, and a new fire hall.

00:10:26.728 --> 00:10:33.680
<v Casey>A number isn't money. Engineers advise Barriere to save $1.5 million a year in reserves, and a town that size doesn't have that.

00:10:34.194 --> 00:10:42.194
<v Riley>But a council that knows its number can turn down a project it can't carry, or price one it wants. That's a choice the council makes for itself.

00:10:42.751 --> 00:10:52.207
<v Casey>A choice between two bad options, if the grants don't come. Kerslake calls grant programs precarious. You don't build a five-year plan on something a budget cycle can cut.

00:10:52.893 --> 00:10:58.717
<v Riley>Then the plan has to say what happens when the grant doesn't arrive. That's the line to look for first.

00:10:58.897 --> 00:10:59.673
<v Casey>Fine. Keep going.

00:11:00.191 --> 00:11:22.583
<v Riley>The next step is where civic voice gets used. Kerslake brought a resolution to the Southern Interior Local Government Association convention. It asks for a broader conversation about how municipal infrastructure is funded, and it names the risk of relying on grant programs that can disappear. That turns one town's problem into a motion other councils can vote on.

00:11:23.241 --> 00:11:33.457
<v Casey>A resolution asks. The article describes a call to start a conversation, not a commitment from the province. Kerslake himself says the gap is an old topic. He's asking others to decide.

00:11:34.069 --> 00:11:51.781
<v Riley>When several councils carry the same complaint, it stops being one mayor's grumble. And the province has a reason to listen. Municipal transfers were cut by $10 million in the 2026/27 budget, from $218 million to $208 million, and mayors criticised that.

00:11:52.307 --> 00:11:58.547
<v Casey>Several councils can agree and still get the same letter back. Who answers a motion from a regional association?

00:11:58.888 --> 00:12:06.480
<v Riley>Whoever the motion is addressed to. The resolution forces that question into the open, and the answer has to come in writing.

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:14.736
<v Casey>In writing, where? Minutes record what a council voted on. They don't record what the province turned down before anything reached the agenda.

00:12:15.101 --> 00:12:19.589
<v Riley>Then the record of a refused request is the gap. That's the fix I'd argue for.

00:12:19.689 --> 00:12:58.751
<v Riley>Now the household side, which is where the money lands. The 2026 Living Wage Report from Living Wage BC and BC Policy Solutions came out on November 19th. It sets the hourly full-time wage a worker needs for a modest standard of living, based on rent, transport, child care, and food. It found no B.C. community where the $18.25 minimum covers that. Whistler has the highest figure at $30.50, Grand Forks the lowest at $22.15. Metro Vancouver is at $28.55. Golden's figure was unchanged at $27.80, and the gap grew in almost every other tracked community.

00:12:59.401 --> 00:13:12.577
<v Casey>That's a modelled number, built from average costs in the big centres. It doesn't measure Barriere's budget, or Williams Lake's, or Quesnel's. But the direction is clear enough. The people who would pay a higher property tax are already short.

00:13:13.237 --> 00:13:23.861
<v Riley>The report measures pressure on residents. Whether Barriere's ratepayers could carry part of a bill is a job for the town's asset plan, and the plan has to show the figures.

00:13:24.517 --> 00:13:31.661
<v Casey>Then the tax math has to be public. A property tax increase modelled behind a closed door is one nobody outside the room can check.

00:13:32.016 --> 00:13:34.304
<v Riley>Agreed. Publish the model with the plan.

00:13:34.873 --> 00:13:41.777
<v Casey>And user fees, the other obvious fix, hit the same households. A water bill is a bill whether you earn a living wage or not.

00:13:42.293 --> 00:13:55.701
<v Riley>It's regressive, which is why the report's other findings matter. It credits the Canadian Dental Care Plan and increases to the BC Rental Assistance Program. Those ease the same squeeze, so the household side isn't only about wages.

00:13:56.377 --> 00:14:01.345
<v Casey>Which is an argument for senior-government transfers. The same transfers Kerslake says are precarious.

00:14:01.978 --> 00:14:07.626
<v Riley>Yes. Federal and provincial help is reaching households, and small towns still can't plan around grant timing.

00:14:07.726 --> 00:14:27.286
<v Riley>Local ownership is the other way to change who decides. TEN is the example I'd point to. We covered its ownership on Thursday. It was set up to make roads safer in Tŝilhqot'in territory and to diversify the local economy after a downturn in forestry. A Nation that owns its own contractor doesn't wait for an outside builder to show up.

00:14:27.893 --> 00:14:40.437
<v Casey>TEN's growth now runs through a partnership with Dawson Civil, a Kamloops firm, and it's working on projects across B.C. Local ownership meant going outside the home territory to grow. A town of 1,000 ratepayers can't do that.

00:14:40.964 --> 00:14:59.676
<v Riley>TEN did have to go outside to grow. But the capacity came first. The Nation holds the majority stake and sets the direction. A council can't copy that overnight, but it can get part of the control through its own procurement rules, a preference for local firms, or shared services with neighbouring councils.

00:15:00.296 --> 00:15:08.984
<v Casey>A procurement rule is still a rule. Rules change when someone with a budget decides they should, and Barriere's council doesn't write the provincial budget.

00:15:09.646 --> 00:15:15.686
<v Riley>It writes its own bylaws, though. Anything the council writes down, it can keep, and the public can check.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:24.488
<v Casey>Writing it down is fine. Who pays when the rule gets ignored? A local firm that loses a bid to a bigger outside firm has no grant to fall back on.

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:33.417
<v Riley>That's a real cost. But Dandzen's partnership is a choice a Nation made after it had built its base. A small council can't copy that in a year.

00:15:34.027 --> 00:15:40.067
<v Casey>So the lesson is the timeline. Years to build capacity, and a town that needs its bridge fixed this spring.

00:15:40.586 --> 00:15:47.722
<v Riley>The bridge is the reason to write the procurement rules now, so local capacity has somewhere to land when the work comes.

00:15:48.267 --> 00:15:59.915
<v Casey>Take the Cariboo version. That parks plan from the roundup is $150,000 that buys a plan. It doesn't buy a waterfront or a trail, and nothing in the coverage says which capital line will pay for either.

00:16:00.447 --> 00:16:09.767
<v Riley>The plan earns its keep when the next capital budget matches it. If the projects the plan names don't appear in the budget, the plan was a press release.

00:16:10.415 --> 00:16:16.791
<v Casey>A city of about 10,700 has a parks plan, a waterfront idea, and a budget that moves one line at a time.

00:16:17.424 --> 00:16:25.264
<v Riley>So the question for Williams Lake isn't whether it needs the plan. It's whether the plan has a line for a grant that doesn't come.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:30.652
<v Casey>I'll believe the plan when there's a line in it for the year the grant doesn't come.

00:16:30.995 --> 00:16:33.611
<v Riley>Then read the capital plan for a line like that.

00:16:34.155 --> 00:16:43.699
<v Casey>Cranbrook's list runs over $40 million across ten years, and $8.3 million of it is Western Financial Place. Somebody wrote that number down, and the council saw it.

00:16:44.351 --> 00:16:54.503
<v Riley>Cranbrook has a bigger tax base than Barriere, and a list that size is still a city's decision. Publishing it means residents see the same number the council sees.

00:16:55.014 --> 00:17:02.782
<v Casey>The list tells you what's wanted. It doesn't tell you what's skipped. The deferred projects are the ones nobody votes on, and they go quiet.

00:17:03.413 --> 00:17:09.133
<v Riley>Then publish both. The wanted list and the deferred list, on the same page, with the same dates.

00:17:09.693 --> 00:17:14.149
<v Casey>Councils don't like publishing the deferred list. That's why it needs to be asked for in public.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:25.053
<v Riley>The provincial finance review is the audience the resolution needs. Whether municipal transfers are on the table in that review matters for every council in the region.

00:17:25.607 --> 00:17:32.135
<v Casey>The tax cut starts January 1st. Nobody has said what a tax cut does to the transfers a town like Barriere counts on.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:37.520
<v Riley>The government can answer that in its own review. I'd want the answer in the same document as the cut.

00:17:37.699 --> 00:17:39.035
<v Casey>Same document, same page.

00:17:39.695 --> 00:17:51.367
<v Riley>So where does the decision sit? The council brings the resolution. The regional association carries it. The province answers, or doesn't. Which of those records counts, I can't settle from these articles.

00:17:52.008 --> 00:17:56.456
<v Casey>The one that gets printed. A refusal that exists only in a letter protects nobody.

00:17:57.037 --> 00:18:04.413
<v Riley>I'd keep the record at the council table, and have the regional association hold a copy. You'd want the province to hold one too.

00:18:04.984 --> 00:18:10.040
<v Casey>Then put all three in the same public place. Otherwise we're back to minutes nobody reads.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:26.938
<v Riley>If a small town's infrastructure depends on grants decided elsewhere, should the record of what it asked for and was refused sit with the town council, the regional association that carries its resolution, or the province, so residents can see where the decision was actually made?

00:18:27.259 --> 00:18:35.459
<v Casey>I'd put that to a council this spring. Pull up your town's last set of minutes, and look for the line that says what happens when the money doesn't come.

00:18:36.017 --> 00:18:40.841
<v Riley>Tell us what you find at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Have a great Saturday.

00:18:41.187 --> 00:18:42.763
<v Casey>Check the pipes before you vote.

00:18:43.363 --> 00:19:00.411
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.